#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218195} :activity/id #uuid "0db9329c-dec7-5a3e-a676-325cf54ff03f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050895 :topic/id #uuid "6f396a30-c21c-5a35-935d-80293083bda2" :topic/math-academy-id 6367} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218195 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218358 :tutorial/content "The Wronskian is a quick way to check whether two solutions of a homogeneous ODE are linearly independent.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn an identity, which tells us how the Wronskian changes with $x$ for an equation of the form \n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the second-order linear homogeneous ODE above on some interval $I.$ Let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & {y}_{2}(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'}(x) & {y}_{2}^{'}(x)\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nThen, **Abel's identity** states that the Wronskian satisfies the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nSolving this equation via separation of variables gives the general form for $W(x){:}$\n\n$$\nW(x) = C e^{-\\int P(x) \\, \\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to determine the behavior of the Wronskian without knowing the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2.$\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''+3xy'+e^x y=0\n$$\n\nwith solutions $y_1$ and $y_2,$ and the corresponding Wronskian $W(x).$ Let's determine the differential equation that $W(x)$ must satisfy.\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{'}(x) & =-3xW(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the equation is not already in standard form, we should rewrite it before applying Abel’s identity." :tutorial/id #uuid "df1133ef-ff8b-5e3c-bbce-c0f1a484db10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14746 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e7527729-b857-5e9b-bea8-a7cbd99e635d" :step/math-academy-id 600667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218196}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218196 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217244 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217003 :question/id #uuid "f6884f33-97b6-502e-91c8-bdd3a4fc95d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21868"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050150}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317378}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "bb9b873b-720d-52a3-af19-21b0736163e7" :step/math-academy-id 600668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218197}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218197 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217004 :question/id #uuid "bac67ae0-dc3c-528c-9e07-7a81caa86db4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21869"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317384}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"} :step/id #uuid "21049594-a386-58b7-948b-933fb27c4926" :step/math-academy-id 600671 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218198 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217005 :question/id #uuid "008cfc63-46d4-5e99-84a2-76df45a51001" :question/math-academy-id "e-21870"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397647}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"} :step/id #uuid "1a5dec47-1b73-5c78-99d1-b02f62ec0319" :step/math-academy-id 600674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218359 :tutorial/content "*Abel's identity* says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their *Wronskian* $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-p(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nTo prove it, we start with the definition\n\n$$\nW(x)=y_1y_2'-y_2y_1'\n$$\n\nand differentiate both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{'}{y}_{2}^{'}}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{''}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{'}{y}_{1}^{'}}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{''} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{''}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{''}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of $y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny_1''=-p(x)y_1'-q(x)y_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_2''=-p(x)y_2'-q(x)y_2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into the expression for $W'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{'}(x) & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{''}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{''} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(-p(x){y}_{2}^{'}-q(x){y}_{2})-{y}_{2}(-p(x){y}_{1}^{'}-q(x){y}_{1}) \\\\  & =-p(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}}+p(x){y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}+\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}} \\\\  & =-p(x)({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}) \\\\  & =-p(x)W(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W'(x)=-p(x)W(x)$, as required." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1263afb-6125-5c5f-8c88-5025fd9457db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14753 :tutorial/title "The Proof of Abel's Identity"} :step/id #uuid "888a5fbb-9e7e-52b5-8de1-71d906dc8972" :step/math-academy-id 600678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218360 :tutorial/content "Because the exponential factor in Abel's identity is *never zero*, we can deduce the following facts:\n\n- If $W(x_0)=0$ at some point $x_0 \\in I,$ then $W(x) = 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\n- If $W(x_0)\\neq 0,$ then $W(x) \\neq 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\nLet's see how this works on concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "1ff5ce47-4514-52a4-9f42-103d6b73b928" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14754 :tutorial/title "Applications of Abel's Identity"} :step/id #uuid "f86b886d-8ad5-5177-85d6-59b75ecbb221" :step/math-academy-id 600679 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218198}}] :activity/title "Abel's Identity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186217326}]}
